<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Koroni</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Coron</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Koroni is a small port town in Messenia, on the south-western coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, guarded by a medieval fortress. Held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> from about 1207 to 1500 as Coron, it was, with nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Modon</a>, one of the 'eyes of the Republic', a vital port of call on the sea route to the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1500.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koroni.jpg" alt="Koroni" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roman Klementschitz, Wien · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koroni.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Koroni was founded in ancient times, and the geographer Pausanias places the original town at modern Petalidi, a few kilometres to the north. The town later moved to its present site, where ancient Asini had stood, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> built a fortress there in the 6th and 7th centuries. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> in 1204 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> passed to Frankish crusaders. A Venetian fleet took the fortress and nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Methoni</a> in 1206 or 1207, and Geoffrey of Villehardouin, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, recognised the possession in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sapienza">Treaty of Sapienza</a> in June 1209.</p><p>For three centuries Coron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Modon</a>, the first Venetian possessions on the Greek mainland, served as a base for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and ships on their way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, and a Venetian document called them the chief eyes of the Republic. Merchants and pilgrims to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> stopped there, and Koroni was noted for cochineal, from which crimson dyes were made. It became the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> bishops, one of whom, Angelo Correr, became Pope Gregory XII. Sultan Bayezid II stormed Methoni in 1500 and Koroni surrendered in August of that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-doria">Andrea Doria</a> retook the town in 1532, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa">Hayreddin Barbarossa</a> forced the Spanish garrison to surrender in 1534. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> recaptured it after a siege from 25 June to 7 August 1685, held it as part of the Kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> until 1715, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> then retook it. Koroni became part of the modern Greek state in 1828, liberated by the French general Nicolas Joseph Maison. Today it is a town of 1,193 people in 2021 on the southwest peninsula of the Peloponnese, on the Gulf of Messinia, below a Venetian castle, and part of the municipality of Pylos-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor</a>. Between 1966 and 1989 a sounding-rocket launch facility near the town sent up 371 rockets.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Koroni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Leo Sgouros held Acrocorinth until his death in 1208, Corinth fell in 1210 and Argos and Nauplia in 1212, while Venice kept the ports of Modon and <b>Coron</b> in the south-west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The advance of the Ottoman Empire cost it Negroponte in 1470, Modon and <b>Coron</b> in 1500, Cyprus in 1571 and Crete in 1669, while the opening of the Portuguese sea route to India after 1498 and the later rise of Dutch and English shipping eroded its position in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Venetian forces occupied Modon (Methoni) and <b>Coron</b> (Koroni) around 1207, and the Treaty of Sapienza with Geoffrey I of Villehardouin of the Principality of Achaea confirmed them in 1209.</blockquote><a href="/state/venetian-modon-and-coron">Venetian Modon and Coron</a></li></ul></section>
